{"@context":["https://w3id.org/ro/crate/1.2-DRAFT/context",{"@vocab":"http://schema.org/"},"http://purl.archive.org/language-data-commons/context.json",{"Geometry":"http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#Geometry","asWKT":"http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#asWKT"},"https://w3id.org/ldac/context"],"@graph":[{"@id":"http://catalog.paradisec.org.au/repository/VKS3","@type":["Data","Object","RepositoryCollection"],"conformsTo":{"@id":"https://w3id.org/ldac/profile#Collection"},"additionalType":"collection","identifier":[{"@id":"#identifier_domain"},{"@id":"#identifier_identifier"},{"@id":"#identifier_collectionIdentifier"},{"@id":"#identifier_doi"}],"name":"Collections of Paul Gardissart","description":"38 audio cassettes of custom stories, cultures and different histories of Vanuatu formally New Hebrides. Paul Gardissart was a former French Teacher from South Africa who came to teach in the New Hebridies. He then resigned and joined Radio Vanuatu producing a Cultural Radio Program until his death in 2016. He was the first presenter to introduce a radio program on custom, culture, and tradition of Vanuatu or New Hebrides on Radio. After his death almost all the recordings that were made by him were officially handed over to the Vanuatu Cultural Centre. Please ask the Vanuatu Cultural Centre for permission to access these files.  Funding supporting the digitisation of these tapes came from the ARC Centre of Excellence for the Dynamics of Language and the Language Data Commons of Australia.","creditText":"Paul Gardissart (collector), 1985. Collections of Paul Gardissart. Collection VKS3 at catalog.paradisec.org.au [Closed Access].  https://dx.doi.org/10.26278/12PT-6M94","contentLocation":[{"@id":"#place-166.0,-21.0-171.0,-13.0"}],"dateCreated":"2022-11-04","dateModified":"2026-01-22","datePublished":"2026-01-22","publisher":{"@id":"http://nla.gov.au/nla.party-1810306"},"collector":{"@id":"#person-12604"},"inLanguage":[{"@id":"http://www.language-archives.org/language/bis